{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction (attention pooling + SWA) is sound, and similar historical experiments show that BiLSTM heads, Focal Loss, and gradient accumulation successfully improved performance, while SWA alone often degraded results. The execution results show a best validation log loss of 0.3514 with overfitting after epoch 9. To address the root cause of overfitting and improve feature extraction, I will refine the plan to replace mean pooling with attention pooling (following successful BiLSTM examples) and implement SWA carefully using only the best checkpoints (avoiding the failed case where all checkpoints were averaged). These changes target the model_design and training_evaluation modules, as they directly impact representation learning and generalization.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current mean pooling + BiLSTM head with a learnable multi-head attention pooling layer that learns to weight token importance for author classification. Specifically, add a transformer-style attention pooling with a learned query vector that computes attention weights over all token embeddings, then aggregate via weighted sum. This better captures authorship-specific stylistic patterns (e.g., archaic words, sentence structure) that mean pooling dilutes. Keep the DistilRoBERTa backbone but remove the BiLSTM to reduce complexity and focus on attention-based aggregation. The forward signature remains (input_ids, attention_mask) -> logits, preserving compatibility with the training loop.", "training_evaluation": "Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) by collecting model state dicts only from the top 3 best validation log loss epochs (instead of all epochs) and using a uniform average. This avoids the failed case where averaging poor checkpoints degraded performance. Additionally, increase gradient clipping to 0.5 (from 1.0) to improve stability, and reduce word dropout probability to 0.05 to prevent excessive masking that may harm learning. Keep early stopping patience at 3. The final inference will use the SWA averaged model. All other training parameters (lr, batch size, accumulation steps) remain unchanged as per the initial plan."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
